		{
			"id": "110227739077920801",
			"parentId": "110227524051252502",
			"url": "https://mastodon.social/@fedbysandrine/110227739040101672",
			"author": {
				"name": "Sandrine",
				"url": "https://mastodon.social/@fedbysandrine",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/301/154/117/521/544/original/df9008be46f71c44.png"
			},
			"date": "2023-04-19T22:03:38.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://toot.cafe/@rich_harris\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>rich_harris</span></a></span> My experience is that people referenced here are also developers.</p><p>I think we all love and are grateful for DX to have become a consideration.<br>The problem is that the focus and argumentation of DX the way it is and has been done, in practice, reinforces the main issue of over-capitalism and does, in fact, harms UX (and the market of UX engineers).</p><p>The Venn labels should be:<br>- people who think we over-proritize DX<br>- people who think we can improve DX while prioritizing UX.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
